<p>Smoke damage claims in San Antonio often start small and grow. A kitchen fire, an electrical fire in a wall, or a fire in a neighboring unit can leave the burned area confined to a few square feet while fine soot and odor spread through the whole building. Smoke residue settles on walls, ceilings, cabinets, clothing, and electronics, works into porous materials like drywall and upholstery, and gets pulled through the HVAC system into rooms that never saw a flame. Because the damage is less visible than charred framing, it is one of the most frequently underestimated parts of a fire claim.</p>
<p>This page explains how smoke claims work, what insurers usually propose, and how a public adjuster in San Antonio can help document the full extent of smoke and soot damage. If your home also has structural fire damage, see <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Where Smoke Damage Hides</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inside closets and cabinets</strong>, where soot settles on stored items and shelving.</li>
<li><strong>Attic insulation and framing</strong>, which absorb odor and can hold fine particles.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC systems</strong>: air handlers, coils, flexible and metal ductwork, and returns.</li>
<li><strong>Electronics and appliances</strong>, where acidic soot can affect circuit boards over time.</li>
<li><strong>Textiles</strong>: carpet, drapes, upholstery, bedding, and clothing.</li>
<li><strong>Wall cavities and electrical boxes</strong>, especially near the origin of an electrical fire.</li>
<li><strong>Exterior surfaces</strong>: stucco, painted trim, and soffits stained by smoke from a nearby fire.</li>
</ul>

