<h2>Smoke Damage in San Antonio Homes and Businesses</h2>
<p>Local construction affects how smoke behaves. Many San Antonio homes have attic air handlers and flexible ductwork running through hot attics, so a fire anywhere in the house can load the whole duct system with soot. Older homes in historic districts such as King William, Monte Vista, and Tobin Hill may have plaster walls, original wood trim, and pier-and-beam crawl spaces where odor lingers and finishes are expensive to restore. Exterior limestone and stucco are porous and can stain when a nearby structure burns.</p>
<p>For businesses, smoke can be as costly as the fire itself. Restaurants, retail stores, and offices may need to discard smoke-exposed inventory, food, and supplies, clean or replace ceiling tiles and HVAC components, and close while the space is restored. Business income coverage, when it applies, may help with lost income during that time. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>, or <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential property claims</a> for homeowners and landlords.</p>

<h2>Clean, Seal, or Replace?</h2>
<p>Most smoke disputes come down to this question. Insurers often start with cleaning, which is appropriate for many surfaces. When cleaning does not remove residue or odor, replacement may be needed.</p>
