<h2>Coverage Questions Specific to Smoke</h2>
<h3>Smoke from a neighboring property</h3>
<p>In duplexes, townhomes, apartment buildings, and shopping centers, smoke from a fire in another unit often reaches yours. Your own policy usually responds to damage to your property and belongings. Renters' policies typically cover the tenant's contents and loss of use, while the building owner's policy covers the structure.</p>
<h3>Wildfire and outdoor smoke</h3>
<p>Smoke that drifts in from a grass or brush fire can raise harder questions, especially when the damage is mostly odor or fine residue. Coverage depends on policy wording and on showing physical damage, so document residue on surfaces and filters soon after the event.</p>
<h3>Common exclusions</h3>
<p>Many policies exclude smoke from agricultural smudging or industrial operations, and damage from long-term sources such as candles, fireplaces, or tobacco use is generally not treated as a sudden loss. Mold or water damage that follows firefighting may fall under separate provisions; see <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Timing Matters With Smoke</h2>
<p>Soot residue can become harder to remove the longer it sits, and some residues can etch or discolor surfaces over time. Report the claim promptly, ask the insurer whether it will authorize emergency cleaning, and keep the mitigation company's notes on what was done and when.</p>

<h2>Documenting a Smoke Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li>Photograph every room, including inside closets, cabinets, and the attic, before cleaning starts.</li>
<li>Wipe-test surfaces with a white cloth and photograph the residue in each room.</li>
<li>Note where odor is present and how strong it is, room by room.</li>
<li>Keep HVAC filters removed after the fire as evidence of soot in the system.</li>
<li>Keep a contents list noting which items were cleaned, and the results.</li>
<li>Save all invoices for cleaning, deodorization, and temporary housing.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has a printable version of these steps.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster Can Help With Smoke Damage</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster represents policyholders, not insurers. On a smoke claim, we may walk every room to map how far smoke traveled, photograph and document residue, review the insurer's cleaning scope against the actual conditions, prepare a line-item estimate for cleaning, sealing, painting, and replacement in Xactimate, and help organize the contents claim. If the insurer's estimate leaves items out, we can request a supplement through the <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> process, and if the disagreement is about the amount of loss, the <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> clause may be an option.</p>
<p>No one can guarantee how an insurer will respond; coverage depends on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. We serve homeowners, landlords, and business owners throughout San Antonio, Bexar County, and surrounding communities.</p>
